From: ysfaculty <haejin.cho@yonsei.ac.kr>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 22:35:55 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 10/13] hbbs: name only its own relay in the messages it
 forwards between clients

hbbs forwarded the relay_server a client wrote: from a controller's
RequestRelay to the controlled PC, which dials it (a ws:// or wss:// value
as it is, from the PC's SYSTEM process), and from a PC's RelayResponse,
PunchHoleSent or LocalAddr to the controller, which dials it too. A
controller with a ticket, or anything that could answer a controller,
could so point the other side at any address. The client's own fix
reaches only the PCs that update; this covers every installed version.

With one relay configured (production runs hbbs -r <domain>), a forwarded
message names that one whatever the sender wrote. Genuine sessions get the
name they get today: an HJViewer build pins its relay-server option to
HJV_SERVER, the domain hbbs is started with, so the PC's RelayResponse,
PunchHoleSent and LocalAddr name it already, and the controller asks for
the relay its punch hole response named. With several configured, a name
in the list passes unchanged and one outside it drops the message, with
a warning "Drop <message> from <addr>: relay server <name> is not
configured" (RequestRelay and RelayResponse also end their connection).
With none configured, names pass as upstream's. Upstream's LAN handling
of RelayResponse comes first, a name equal to hbbs's own LAN address
(--mask) is its own, and a RelayResponse acknowledging a RequestRelay
still names nothing.

The relays are taken as configured, not as upstream's list of those
that resolved when hbbs started: a relay domain that did not resolve
then (DNS not up yet after a reboot) would leave that list empty and the
names unchanged, with nothing in the log but "relay-servers=[]".
test_if_valid_server returned before its error line when the name did
not resolve; it now logs "Invalid relay-servers <name>: ..." (ERROR).

tests/untrusted_input.rs: a punch hole, the PC's relay response and
punch hole report, another controller's relay request and its
acknowledgement, all naming another relay or the configured one, with
one, two and no relays configured, and with one whose name does not
resolve.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
